From 128320d3ab23816b0d6d5c3f191a1b302615400e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Gomez <emmanuel@gomez.io>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 09:22:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(gw): TLS + password-file auth for the Postgres
 backend

This build shipped passwordless local-peer/NoTls-only, which cannot
reach a CloudNativePG cluster over the network. Add two out-of-band,
file-based inputs so the gateway can authenticate with SCRAM over TLS
without ever accepting a secret via env or URL (those paths stay
rejected).

Password file (DOCUMENTDB_PG_PASSWORD_FILE):
- New env key; read after the existing password-rejection guards run
  (PGPASSWORD, in-URL, and JSON PostgresDataUserPassword remain
  rejected). A single trailing newline is trimmed.
- Populates postgres_data_user_password AND the new
  postgres_system_user_password field. The system/bootstrap pool
  authenticates as the separate postgres_system_user role, which had no
  password field and passed None; it now threads the system password in
  get_system_connection_pool.

Backend TLS (DOCUMENTDB_PG_TLS_CA_FILE):
- New env key + postgres_tls_ca_file field (distinct from the
  Mongo-facing listener CA in CertificateOptions.ca_path).
- make_pg_tls_connector builds an openssl tls_client connector with
  PEER verification, loading the CA file when set and otherwise using
  the system trust store. Replaces the NoTls argument in the backend
  connection pools; deadpool's Manager type-erases the TLS type so no
  pool signatures change.

New trait accessors postgres_system_user_password() and
postgres_tls_ca_file() are added to SetupConfiguration. The manual Debug
impl redacts the new system password. Covered by unit tests in
setup_tests.rs and connection_pool.rs.

Authored against DOCUMENTDB_TAG=v0.114-0.
